1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,950
Hello, I'm Hayden.

2
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:06,750
I'm one of the archaeologists investigating the site here at Bidcaster.

3
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:09,860
This is the third summer for this community project

4
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:13,670
and most of the people digging here are volunteers.

5
00:00:13,670 --> 00:00:16,880
I'm a full-time archaeologist for the town council

6
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:20,676
but I was asked to join the project by NHA,

7
00:00:20,676 --> 00:00:25,470
a charity which sets up projects like this up and down the country.

8
00:00:25,470 --> 00:00:28,640
As you can see, we're next to Bidcaster Castle

9
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:33,650
which is great because the owners let us use their facilities.

10
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:37,490
So, how did we get to where we are today?

11
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:42,025
Many archaeology projects happen when an ancient object is found,

12
00:00:42,707 --> 00:00:46,740
and in our case, that object was a gold coin

13
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:54,095
Coins are often found by people using metal detectors to look for things buried in the ground -

14
00:00:54,095 --> 00:00:59,720
or coins are uncovered when wild animals like rabbits have been digging tunnels.

15
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:07,350
Here, a walker found it on the ground after a rainstorm washed away some of the earth and sand.

16
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:13,530
When the story of the gold coin hit the news, Peter Swift, an amateur historian,

17
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:18,140
contacted me to say he believed there had been a village on this site

18
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:21,770
centuries before the castle was built.

19
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:27,868
Just by chance, the team found some old maps and documents in our library

20
00:01:27,868 --> 00:01:37,450
which showed five-hundred-year-old drawings of ruined buildings on the grassy area between the outer stone walls of the castle and the river.

21
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:41,360
We knew then we were onto something.

22
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,708
Over the three summers the team has been here,

23
00:01:44,708 --> 00:01:46,620
we've found the remains of several buildings

24
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:50,660
and more broken pots than you can count!

25
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:54,580
Normally you'd expect to find brooches and other jewellery

26
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:58,710
but we're still waiting to uncover any such items.

27
00:01:58,710 --> 00:02:04,152
The people who once lived here were skilled at making tools from animal bones,

28
00:02:04,152 --> 00:02:07,650
as you'll see when you visit the exhibition.

29
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:09,930
Besides the discovery of the village,

30
00:02:09,930 --> 00:02:15,630
we've also found evidence of human activity on the other side of the river -

31
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:18,120
no other houses or huts so far

32
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:22,470
but we can see the borders of an ancient field system.

33
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:26,740
At one point we found a long wall and thought it was an ancient palace

34
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:30,570
but it turned out to be a modern wall.

35
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:35,450
This summer's work will end soon, but we'll be back next summer.

36
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:42,410
In the meantime, we're putting on a series of guided tours for school groups this autumn.

37
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:47,340
Oh and, maybe you saw the TV documentary about our project.

38
00:02:47,340 --> 00:02:51,640
That suggested the objects we've found are going to the town's museum

39
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:54,940
but we don't know that for sure yet.

40
00:02:54,940 --> 00:03:00,830
When you enter the site, please make sure you keep to the paths at all times.

41
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:04,660
There are a few other things - the highlights of the site

42
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:07,130
if you like - that I want to mention.

43
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:13,320
Take a look at the map; our present location is marked at the bottom.

44
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:18,310
This year we've identified the foundations of an ancient bridge.

45
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:20,160
And it's really exciting today

46
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:25,970
because a team of divers are in the river searching for lost objects.

47
00:03:25,970 --> 00:03:30,402
To reach the bridge, take the main path ahead of you,

48
00:03:30,402 --> 00:03:35,640
go straight on and keep going till the path bends to the left.

49
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:39,580
You'll see a smaller track leading off to the right.

50
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:44,560
Follow that to take you to the river, where the divers are.

51
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:47,690
You might be interested to see the rubbish pit.

52
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:53,460
This is very near the castle walls in the northwest corner of the site.

53
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:58,700
It actually dates to the time of the castle and not the ancient village.

54
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:01,380
We found oyster shells and fish bones

55
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:07,210
and we assume they were thrown from the castle kitchen above.

56
00:04:07,210 --> 00:04:13,320
One area we excavated in the first summer uncovered the site of a meeting hall.

57
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:19,034
We knew it was an important building because it had two rows of post holes,

58
00:04:19,034 --> 00:04:21,700
deep enough to support a large roof.

59
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:25,800
It's the largest structure in the central area of the site

60
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:29,320
next to the current excavation area.

61
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:33,560
Last year we discovered a fish pond in the ancient village.

62
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:36,350
Normally these were beside a river.

63
00:04:36,350 --> 00:04:42,730
The pond here is further away, but it's possible the river has moved slightly.

64
00:04:42,730 --> 00:04:45,335
Anyway, to get there from here,

65
00:04:45,335 --> 00:04:49,700
you turn right at the first information board you come to

66
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:53,150
and follow the path into the trees.

67
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:57,580
Before you come out of the trees, you'll see it on your right.

68
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:01,730
If you reach the river, you've gone too far.

69
00:05:01,730 --> 00:05:04,280
So, does anyone...
